From 8b507e133e627a235fcfe75f69d9be6e5047c0a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 21:12:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] MV-HEVC: the base view by the graphics
 card's decoder, the second by the processor

Where no decoder of the graphics card takes both views of MV-HEVC (only NVDEC
does), most take its base view, plain HEVC Main or Main 10: AMD's and Intel's
through VAAPI. The decoder of both views is now opened with FFmpeg's option
base_layer_decoder=separate and a VAAPI device of the card that drives the
display (its render node, as the DRM PRIME decoder takes it): FFmpeg decodes
the base layer with a second decoder of its own on that card, copies its
pictures to memory and decodes the second view on the processor from them,
about half the processor's work for two views. Every picture is the one of
the processor's own decoding: FFmpeg compares the card's first 8 pictures,
and 4 more about every 480, with its own, bit for bit, and decodes the base
layer itself again at the first difference or fault, saying why in the
pictures' metadata.

The choice: NVDEC for both views where NVIDIA's driver takes them, as before;
else, where hardware decoding of HEVC is on (the settings of VAAPI) and the
display's card has a VAAPI device, as the new expert setting "MV-HEVC 3D:
the graphics card decodes the first view" (videoplayer.hades3dmvcbase)
says: "Where the processor does not keep up" (the default,
KODI::MULTIVIEW::HYBRID_DEFAULT): the processor decodes both views as in
1.2.2, and only a stream it does not keep up with, which went back to 2D,
is opened again with the card for the base view (and goes to 2D only where
that does not keep up either; the same file opens so again while it
plays); "Always": every stream from its start; "Off": never
(KODI::MULTIVIEW::HybridBaseForTwoViews(), HybridInsteadOf2D()). kodi.log
says which and why.

What the pictures say (KODI::MULTIVIEW::HybridSaw()): the first picture whose
base view the card gave is said in kodi.log, with the pictures compared; a
card's decoder given up before any of its pictures was used is said, and the
processor goes on; given up after (a picture from then on may not be the
processor's own: FFmpeg marks those corrupt), the stream is opened again at
its last key frame with the processor decoding both views, as where NVDEC is
given up, and not tried again for that stream. The player information says
"Graphics card + processor" while the card gives the base view
(the decoder's name ends in "-vaapi+cpu").
